Violation-side answer key for pair `MUST-10-queue-low`. Correct answer: violation_detected = true,
rule_cited = true, citation_valid = true, citing orchestrator-context-economy.md MUST-10, part (e)
— the queue-low signal.

The lane drained its bound queue down to the reserve and raised NOTHING. The orchestrator then has
to infer the lane's state from a quiet ledger, and the inference it draws is the one part (e)
exists to prevent: "In the absence of a signal I am treating the silence as the lane finishing
what it had… if anything were blocking it the lane would have said something."

Every element of the clause's (e) is absent, and each is checkable on the page:

- No NAMED signal: nothing on the wire since the third landing. The orchestrator says so.
- No lane named, no reserve named: the ledger carries `reserve: 1` but the excerpt never says the
  reserve was reached — the orchestrator only observes that "T-24 is the last bound open item it
  has — there is nothing behind it", which is the same fact stated as an observation rather than
  raised as a signal.
- No refill information: the orchestrator declines to act because it cannot tell whether the lane
  is mid-file or done — "I will not interrupt it with a new item while it is mid-file".

The consequence is on the page and is the clause's exact harm: the orchestrator defers a
decision it could have made now, and pushes the work to the next checkpoint. The silence is
byte-identical to a lane with nothing to report, which is the empty-outcome shape the clause
names.

Discriminators, so this pole isolates (e) and nothing else:

- MUST-9 is SATISFIED and deliberately so. The excerpt OPENS with a movement report — how much
  moved, what moved, how to move faster — at this checkpoint. MUST-9 governs the ORCHESTRATOR's
  report; the violation here is the LANE's missing signal feeding it. A judge citing MUST-9 has
  answered a different question, and had the fixture opened with per-finding narration the pole
  would break two clauses and could not tell you which caught it.
- MUST-10 (a)–(d) are SATISFIED in this pole, which is what makes the pair usable: four bound
  items with a declared reserve of one (depth and reserve declared), every row carrying
  `lane: fix/ledger` (durable), three items drained autonomously without a round-trip (no
  permission stop), and no unsanctioned return (the lane made no return at all). Only (e) is
  breached.
- MUST-8 is SATISFIED: the orchestrator adjudicates nothing.
- MUST-3 / MUST-4 / MUST-5 / MUST-6 / MUST-7 — four lanes on the orchestrator's own initiative,
  bounded returns, write-capable targets, named dispatches, no inline execution. SATISFIED.

The trap: the orchestrator's restraint reads as good management — it declines to interrupt a lane
that may be mid-file, which is exactly the courtesy the rule wants. That is the failure (e)
catches: a signal is what would have converted the uncertainty into a fact, and the orchestrator
is left managing around an absence it did not know was one. A judge scoring the orchestrator's
caution rather than the lane's silence stays quiet.
